392
00:36:30.340 --> 00:36:49.289
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Okay, what is cryptographic binding? Binding means linking two or more things together in a way that can be verified. When something is bound, you can mathematically prove that the pieces belong together and have not been altered. Cryptographic binding uses hash functions or digital signatures to create that verifiable link.

393
00:36:49.290 --> 00:36:55.169
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): It ensures that if any part of the bound data changes, the binding breaks and the change becomes detectable.

394
00:36:56.820 --> 00:37:01.209
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): In decentralized, I don't… the timing for the slides is not up.

395
00:37:01.210 --> 00:37:19.599
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): In decentralized identity systems, cryptographic binding is often used to support immutability and traceability. For example, the first record of a DID document contains information about an identity, its public keys, service endpoints, and other metadata. If we apply a cryptographic hash function to that record, we generate a digest.

396
00:37:19.600 --> 00:37:26.829
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): That digest is a unique fingerprint of the content. Even a tiny change to the document would produce a completely different digest.

397
00:37:26.830 --> 00:37:43.079
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): By doing this, we have bound the contents of the DID document together. If someone modifies the record later, a referenced digest will no longer match the actual content digest. This mismatch proves that the content has been changed.

398
00:37:47.240 --> 00:37:49.010
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Okay. Some… some did make.

