401
00:38:07.730 --> 00:38:15.050
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Binding can also continue over time. Suppose the identity rotates its keys and publishes a new version of its DID document.

402
00:38:15.050 --> 00:38:20.650
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): While the DID specification itself does not require cryptographic chaining between versions.

403
00:38:20.650 --> 00:38:37.759
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Some did methods implement it. In methods that used hash-chain storage, such as didWebVH and DIDWEBS, the new record includes the digest of the previous record. This creates a verifiable sequence where each update is cryptographically linked to the one before.

404
00:38:38.240 --> 00:38:57.379
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Now, the identity history becomes tamper-evident. If someone attempts to rewrite an earlier record, every subsequent digest in the chain would fail to verify. The binding does not prevent change, but it makes unauthorized change detectable. Well, it just makes… it makes change detectable, doesn't have to be unauthorized. Okay.

405
00:38:57.380 --> 00:38:59.529
